1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:02,610
if, for example, in an online shop, someone

2
00:00:02,610 --> 00:00:03,750
clicks place order,

3
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:05,320
and maybe the page

4
00:00:05,430 --> 00:00:07,700
feels a little slow, they click it again

5
00:00:07,810 --> 00:00:09,440
now our server gets

6
00:00:09,830 --> 00:00:12,140
two requests, and it creates two orders

7
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:14,090
and if we charge on each order, you can

8
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:15,210
charge the user twice

9
00:00:15,210 --> 00:00:16,710
it doesn't even need a double-click

10
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:18,470
the phone can lose the network after the

11
00:00:18,470 --> 00:00:20,370
request was sent and the app can retry

12
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:22,390
or they can refresh the page, and then the

13
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:23,630
browser asks them if they want to send the

14
00:00:23,630 --> 00:00:25,300
form again and they say yes

15
00:00:25,390 --> 00:00:27,570
disabling the button after the first click

16
00:00:27,570 --> 00:00:29,250
helps with this, but it only covers the

17
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:30,810
double-click part it does nothing for like

18
00:00:30,810 --> 00:00:32,790
maybe a retry or a second tab

19
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:35,170
the fix that is common for this is using an

20
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:35,690
idempotency

21
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:36,590
key

22
00:00:36,930 --> 00:00:37,990
basically, an idempotency

23
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:40,110
key puts things in place such

24
00:00:40,290 --> 00:00:43,190
that doing something twice has the same

25
00:00:43,190 --> 00:00:45,170
result, as doing it just once

26
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:48,710
stripe's API uses the same idea with an

27
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:49,990
idempotency key header

28
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:51,130
so a retried

29
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:53,000
request doesn't create a second payment
